  "response": "What ultimately closed the dispute was the recovery of adult material and its systematic appraisal by comparative anatomists of international standing. Robert Broom recovered adult australopithecine fossils at Sterkfontein beginning in 1936, and only afterwards did Wilfrid Le Gros Clark examine the South African australopithecine fossils in 1947. The adult cranial and dental material from Sterkfontein shows that the australopithecines were hominids rather than apes, which is exactly the conclusion reached by the 1947 Pan-African Congress on Prehistory in Nairobi; and Le Gros Clark's firsthand examination of the fossils in 1947 brought about that verdict, since his endorsement carried the authority that converted the assembled prehistorians. With adult specimens in hand, the appeal to juvenile resemblance lost its force, and the hominid status of Australopithecus became the settled view of the discipline.\n\nQuestions of absolute antiquity were also brought to bear on the status of the specimen. Radiocarbon dating of the Taung cave deposits established an age of about 2.8 million years, a figure that framed all subsequent discussion of where the Taung child stood relative to later fossil forms.\n\nThe anatomical debate turned in large part on the immaturity of the Taung specimen. The Taung individual was a juvenile at the time of death; in other words, it died before reaching adulthood. Precisely because the individual died before reaching adulthood, Arthur Keith argued that the Taung skull belonged to a juvenile anthropoid ape, whose diagnostic adult characters had simply not yet formed. The dentition of the specimen is well characterized in at least one respect, and perhaps in two: the canine teeth of the Taung individual were small and non-projecting, or the dental arcade of the Taung individual lacked the diastema seen in apes. Keith's identification of the specimen as a juvenile anthropoid ape cannot be squared with the forward position of the foramen magnum in the Taung skull, which indicated an upright posture; a quadrupedal ape and a creature carrying its head over an erect vertebral column cannot both be descriptions of the same cranial base, and the basicranial evidence rules the ape reading out.\n\nThe adjudication of the australopithecine question proceeded not through a single decisive stroke but through a sequence of publications, anatomical arguments and comparative study of later material. Raymond Dart described the Taung skull in the journal Nature in February 1925, after the fossil had been recovered from a limestone quarry in Tanzania. That his paper bore the title \"Australopithecus africanus: The Man-Ape of South Africa\" confirms that he had assigned the Taung specimen to the new species Australopithecus africanus.",
